@page "/getting-started/packages"

@* The Packages page (ADR-0110), a reference page: the family's packages by product, in ADR-0042's table
   order, which the build checks is exactly what release.yml publishes. Each description is the
   package's assembly's, which is the text nuget.org shows, and what each brings with it is read from
   its project. Nothing here is typed twice. *@
<DocsPage Title="Packages"
          Lead="The family's packages by product, with what each one is and what it brings with it. Add the products you use, and in a MudBlazor application each one's Wrapper; every package of the family is installed at one version.">

    <DocsSection Title="The packages">
        <ReferenceAsOf>
            Each description is the one nuget.org shows, read from the package's own assembly, and the list is the
            one the release publishes from a tag.
        </ReferenceAsOf>
        @foreach (var product in Packages.ByProduct)
        {
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" HtmlTag="h3" Class="docs-reference-heading">
                @if (Packages.PageOf(product.Key) is { } productRoute)
                {
                    <a href="@productRoute">@product.Key</a>
                }
                else
                {
                    @product.Key
                }
            </MudText>
            <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table docs-reference-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th style="width: 22%">Package</th><th>What it is</th><th style="width: 22%">Brings with it</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var package in product)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <a href="@package.NuGetUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>@package.Id</code></a>
                                <div><a href="@package.ReadmeUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="docs-muted docs-reference-small">Readme</a></div>
                            </td>
                            <td>@package.Description</td>
                            <td>
                                @if (package.DependsOn.Count == 0)
                                {
                                    <span class="docs-muted">No package of the family</span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    @for (var k = 0; k < package.DependsOn.Count; k++)
                                    {
                                        @(k > 0 ? ", " : "")<code>@package.DependsOn[k]</code>
                                    }
                                }
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </MudSimpleTable>
        }
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Which to install">
        <p>
            Add the package of each product you use. NuGet brings what it depends on, so a product's component is
            one line:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>A grid</b>: <code>ExGrid</code>. It brings @Brings("ExGrid").</li>
            <li><b>A sheet</b>: <code>ExSheet</code>. It brings @Brings("ExSheet").</li>
            <li><b>A pivot table</b>: <code>ExPivot</code>. It brings @Brings("ExPivot").</li>
            <li><b>A chart</b>: <code>ExChart</code>. It brings @Brings("ExChart").</li>
            <li>
                <b>In a MudBlazor application</b>, add each product's Wrapper too: <code>ExGrid.MudBlazor</code>,
                <code>ExSheet.MudBlazor</code>, <code>ExPivot.MudBlazor</code>, <code>ExChart.MudBlazor</code>. A Wrapper draws the product's menus,
                panels and editors with MudBlazor's controls, and never changes what the product does. See the
                <a href="features#what-each-wrapper-draws">Feature matrix</a> for what each one draws.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>On a server, with no UI</b>: <code>ExSheet.Engine</code> computes a Sheet Document's Values and
                <code>ExPivot.Engine</code> a pivot report, by the same rules as the screen, and <code>ExChart.Engine</code> lays out a chart's scene; <code>ExGrid.Data</code>
                holds the data as a Snapshot.
            </li>
            <li><b>Apache Arrow</b>: <code>ExGrid.Data.Arrow</code> reads a Snapshot from Arrow and writes one as Arrow.</li>
        </ul>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/install.sh" />
        <p>
            Each package's readme, linked above, gives its stylesheet component and a first page. The
            <a href="getting-started">Installation</a> page puts the first grid on a page.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="One version for the family">
        <p>
            Every package of the family is published at one version, from one tag. Each depends on <b>exactly</b>
            the version of the others it was built and tested with: the range is <code>[x]</code>, not
            <code>&gt;= x</code>. So upgrade them together.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                An application that upgrades one package and not another is told at restore, never left running a
                Wrapper against a core it never met: <b>NU1608</b> where the application references both packages
                directly, and <b>NU1107</b> where two of its dependencies ask for different versions.
            </li>
            <li>
                Every version so far is a prerelease, published before the Definition of Done is signed off: a beta,
                <code>0.1.0-beta.N</code>, or a release candidate, <code>0.1.0-rc.N</code>, which the sign-off is made
                against. NuGet offers one only when prereleases are asked for: <code>--prerelease</code>, or
                the box that includes them. A version without a suffix waits for the sign-off.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="docs-reference-cites">
            Decided in <a href="@DocsBuild.BlobUrl(Packages.FamilyRecordPath)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ADR-0042</a>.
            What each version holds is in the <a href="release-notes">Release notes</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>

@code {
    // What a product's component package depends on, in words: "ExSheet.Engine and ExGrid".
    private static MarkupString Brings(string id)
    {
        var dependsOn = Packages.All.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Id == id)?.DependsOn ?? [];
        var names = dependsOn.Select(d => $"<code>{System.Net.WebUtility.HtmlEncode(d)}</code>").ToList();
        var words = names.Count switch
        {
            0 => "no other package of the family",
            1 => names[0],
            _ => string.Join(", ", names.Take(names.Count - 1)) + " and " + names[^1],
        };
        return new MarkupString(words);
    }
}
